  "title": "The Summer the Capital Emptied",
  "subtitle": "Yellow Fever, Race, and the Collapse of Philadelphia in 1793",
  "authorDisplayName": "Liszt Think",
  "description": "In August 1793, yellow fever struck Philadelphia, then the nation’s capital. Within weeks, George Washington fled and half the city followed. Five thousand died before the first frost. Amid the exodus, Dr. Benjamin Rush bled patients by doctrine while French physician Jean Devèze and merchant Stephen Girard transformed Bush Hill hospital into a sanctuary of practical care. Absalom Jones and Richard Allen led the Free African Society in nursing the sick and burying the dead under a false promise of immunity, only to face public condemnation after the crisis passed. From waterfront wharves to the Committee of Twenty’s ledgers, this narrative reconstructs a city governed by volunteers when federal authority vanished. Drawing on letters, burial returns, and contemporary accounts, it reveals how medical dogma, racial prejudice, and civic duty collided in America’s first great public health catastrophe.",
